Q: JAR runs correctly from command line but not by double-clicking A: <p>Please look at the old answers, but here are more issues.</p>
<h3>The Basics</h3>
<p>The &quot;file&quot; is inside the jar, which is a form of zip file.</p>
<p>This means that <code>File</code> (for a disk file) is not usable, but the generalized <code>Path</code> could also be used. Also an <code>URL/URI</code> would be feasible.</p>
<p>Under the hood <strong>binary</strong> read (of bytes) can be done with an <code>InputStream</code>.</p>
<p>Reading as text can be done with a <code>Reader</code> using some <code>Charset</code> encoding on an InputStream.</p>
<h3>Getting the resource</h3>
<p>In your <code>com.steve.Testing class the resource </code>com.resources.testfile.txt` cannot be accessed by a relative path, but:</p>
<pre><code>URL url = getClass().getResource(&quot;/com/resources/testfile.txt&quot;);
Path path = Paths.get(url.toURI());
</code></pre>
<p>This uses <code>URI</code>, a newer class than <code>URL</code>. <code>Path</code> is generalized, and maintains a file system it is using, in this case a Zip file system internally.</p>
<pre><code>private String getTextFromFile() throws IOException {
    URL url = getClass().getResource(&quot;/com/resources/testfile.txt&quot;);
    Path path = Paths.get(url.toURI());
    String content = Files.readString(path);
    System.out.println(content);
    return content;
}
</code></pre>
<p>As you see, the <code>Files</code> class has very nice functonality, for instance to do:</p>
<pre><code>Path path2 = Paths.get(&quot;...&quot;);
Files.copy(path, path2);
</code></pre>
<p>In this way your read-only resource file may be copied for editing.</p>
<p>Using <code>String</code> and <code>concat</code>/<code>+=</code> might be cleverly optimized by the compiler, but still is not a good idea for larger files.</p>
<p><strong>HOWEVER</strong> This code assumes the file has encoding UTF-8. Fine for Ubuntu.</p>
<p>You could pick an other encoding but UTF-8 is superior, by providing the <code>Charset</code>.</p>
<pre><code>    Charset charset = StandardCharsets.ISO_889_1;
    String content = Files.readString(pat, charset);
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>System.out.println</code> again converts the String (which internally is always Unicode, UTF-8/UTT-16) to the running platform encoding. Say on a Windows Machine maybe Greek script.</p>
